Legend:
- Gl : direct presentation of the symbol (as rendered in vscode)
- FN : File in which the keyseq for the symbol is defined
  - 01   01_up.xcompose
  - 03   03_down.xcompose
  - 05   05_left.xcompose
  - 07   07_right.xcompose
  - 10   10_equal.xcompose
  - 11   11_comma.xcompose
  - 12   12_period.xcompose
  - 13   13_backslash.xcompose
  - 14   14_slash.xcompose
  - 15   15_minus.xcompose
  - 21   21_descriptive.xcompose
  - 23   23_misc.xcompose
  - 41   41_doublekey.xcompose
  - 43   43_doubletap.xcompose
- 'keyseq' always begins with the 'Compose' key ('⎄'), i.e. (either) Alt ('⎇')
- 'keyseq' is a sequence of key strokes, hit one after the other
- 'keyseq' has the head (including '⎄') and a tail
  - head always begins with '⎄'
  - then contains 'modifiers'
  - then the 'semantic' carrier key
  - then coda
  - e∙g the glyph ≃ is ⎄ - ↑ ` = compose, minus, up, grave
- 'leader' keys are unshifted, easily accessible keys
- all keyseq begin with the Compose key, `⎄`
  - formally: '<Multi_key>'
  - set to either 'Alt' key, '⎇'
- Common leader keys
  - comma, ','
  - dot, '.'
  - backslash, '\'
  - colon, ':'
  - apostrophe, '
  - slash, '/'
  - equals, '='
  - minus, '-'
  - Other Leader Schemes
    - double compose then seq tail (e.g. ⎄ ⎄ o)
    - compose, double-tap (e.g. ⎄ o o)
- Common modifier keys
  - '↑' <Up>
  - '↓' <Down>
  - '←' <Left>
  - '→' <Right>
